            "<strong>Sepsis</strong> is life-threatening organ dysfunction caused by a dysregulated host response to infection — the body's own inflammation, coagulation and vasodilatation damaging organs far from the infected site. <strong>Septic shock</strong> is the subset with circulatory and cellular failure: hypotension needing a vasopressor to keep the MAP at 65 mmHg or above <em>and</em> a lactate above 2 mmol/L despite adequate fluid, with a hospital mortality around 40 per cent. The 2016 Sepsis-3 definitions replaced the old 'SIRS' criteria (fever, tachycardia, tachypnoea, white cell count), which were sensitive but non-specific, with <strong>organ dysfunction</strong> measured by a rise of two or more points in the <strong>SOFA score</strong> — which grades respiration (PaO₂/FiO₂), coagulation (platelets), liver (bilirubin), cardiovascular (MAP and vasopressor dose), brain (GCS) and kidney (creatinine and urine output). At the bedside outside the ICU, <strong>qSOFA</strong> — respiratory rate 22 or more, altered mentation, systolic pressure 100 mmHg or less; two of three means high risk — and the early warning score are the screens; neither is sensitive enough to exclude sepsis, so a nurse who thinks 'could this be sepsis?' in any patient with a possible infection and any new deterioration is right to act. Sepsis is the commonest reason for admission to and death in intensive care in Ghana as everywhere, and the sources — pneumonia, the urinary tract, the abdomen (perforation, typhoid, appendicitis), the skin and soft tissue, the puerperal uterus and the septic abortion, meningitis, malaria and the lines and tubes the unit itself inserted — are sought in every case because the antibiotic that does not reach the source does not work.",
