            "Half the patients in an intensive care unit develop <strong>acute kidney injury</strong> (AKI), and the kidney is usually the victim of what brought them in — sepsis, shock, haemorrhage, severe malaria, rhabdomyolysis, obstetric haemorrhage and eclampsia, herbal remedies, nephrotoxic drugs and contrast. KDIGO stages it by creatinine and urine output: stage 1 is a rise of 26.5 micromol/L in 48 hours or 1.5 to 1.9 times baseline, or urine output below 0.5 mL/kg/hour for 6 to 12 hours; stage 2 is 2 to 2.9 times baseline or oliguria for 12 hours or more; stage 3 is three times baseline, a creatinine above 354 micromol/L, urine below 0.3 mL/kg/hour for 24 hours, anuria for 12 hours, or the start of dialysis. The nurse's first job is to <strong>locate the fault</strong>: pre-renal (the starved kidney — concentrated urine, a history of loss or shock, responds to restored circulation), intrinsic renal (the damaged kidney — gentamicin, NSAIDs, contrast, glomerulonephritis, myoglobin, haemoglobinuria of blackwater fever, prolonged pre-renal injury), or post-renal (the blocked kidney — the catheter kinked or obstructed, the enlarged prostate, the cervical tumour, bilateral stones; sudden complete anuria is obstruction until proved otherwise, and the catheter is flushed or changed before anything else). The second job is <strong>prevention of the second hit</strong>: perfusion restored (MAP 65 or the patient's own higher baseline in chronic hypertension), the drug chart reviewed daily for nephrotoxins and doses adjusted to the estimated clearance, contrast avoided or preceded by hydration, intra-abdominal pressure measured when the abdomen is tense, and fluid given to the hypovolaemic and <em>withheld</em> from the overloaded — because the oliguric patient drowned in 'renal-dose' fluid is as common a death as the one left dry. Furosemide does not prevent or treat AKI; it is used only to manage overload in the patient who still responds to it, and 'low-dose dopamine' belongs to history. Hourly urine output and daily weight on the same scale are the two observations that matter most.",
